Implement IOleWindow in wxIAccessible.

WindowFromAccessibleObject() finds the window of an IAccessible by asking it
for IOleWindow and, when the object doesn't implement it, by repeating the
question up the get_accParent() chain. wxIAccessible only implemented
IAccessible, so the lookup always ended at the first object above it that did,
which in practice is the standard accessible object of an ancestor window.
Every wx accessible object of a child window was therefore reported as
belonging to its parent's HWND.

The most visible consequence is in UI Automation clients (Narrator,
Accessibility Insights, axe-windows), which see MSAA servers through a proxy:
the proxy lists the container's IAccessible children and the child HWNDs
separately unless it can tell that a child object belongs to a child window, so
every child window carrying a wxAccessible appeared twice in the UIA tree, once
as the HWND and once as a nameless-id copy, and a selected page tab, for
instance, was flagged as having a selected sibling. Screen readers using
IAccessible directly did not see the duplicate.

Derive wxIAccessible from IOleWindow as well and return the HWND of the
associated wxWindow from GetWindow(). QueryInterface() is written out instead
of using IMPLEMENT_IUNKNOWN_METHODS() because with two base interfaces it has
to hand out the pointer to the requested subobject rather than "this"; AddRef()
and Release() are the macro's bodies verbatim.

Submitted upstream as a pull request against wxWidgets master, where the file
is identical to 3.3.2; drop this patch once Orca picks up a wx release that
contains it.

